---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portrait from '../assets/timur.jpg';
---

<section
  id="founder-note"
  class="mx-auto max-w-page border-t border-border px-6 py-section-sm md:px-8 md:py-section"
  aria-labelledby="founder-note-heading"
>
  <h2 id="founder-note-heading" class="sr-only">A note from the founder</h2>

  <!--
    The max-width grows at md by exactly the photo column (7rem photo + 1.5rem gap). Without that,
    the photo takes its width out of the quote rather than out of the page, and the measure drops
    below the point where a paragraph stays comfortable to read.
  -->
  <figure
    data-reveal
    class="max-w-[62ch] rounded-r-[var(--radius-card)] border-l border-accent bg-surface-accent p-6 md:max-w-[calc(62ch+8.5rem)] md:p-8"
  >
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-5 md:flex-row md:gap-6">
      <!--
        shrink-0 matters: without it the flex item is allowed to compress and the circle renders as
        an ellipse once the quote competes for width.
      -->
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Timur Jalilov"
        width={112}
        height={112}
        densities={[2]}
        format="webp"
        loading="lazy"
        class="size-28 shrink-0 rounded-full border border-accent object-cover"
      />

      <blockquote>
        <p class="text-lead text-text">
          I spent years as a senior front-end engineer before starting Tandempoint. That is the
          reason it works this way: I can read your documentation and tell whether a creator will
          have a good hour with your product, and I can watch a creator's back catalogue and tell
          whether they know what they are talking about. It also means there is no account-manager
          layer here. You talk to me, and I talk to the creators. Small and direct is the point,
          not a limitation.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </div>

    <figcaption class="mt-6 text-small text-muted">Timur Jalilov, Founder</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
